 change  we are just rearranging the deck chairs  on houseboats at lakes mead and pal  we have to understand that we have seen  massive reductions in flows in the  colorado river  over the past 20 years we've seen 20  percent decline we are likely to see an  additional 20 percent decline in flows  in the coming years  and we have to understand that  uh man-made systems anthropogenic  climate change human-caused climate  change is exacerbating that and driving  that  and until we figure out how to really  cut emissions in a serious way i know  congress has taken some recent actions  to do that but congress has also taken  recent actions to  lease uh every year an additional 62  million acres for oil and gas leasing  so  we we inherently contradict ourselves  i'm hearing inherent contradictions and  everybody's talking points on the river  and uh and so we got to come to grips  with with reality but i think those  three things understanding groundwater  understanding our food supply and  understanding how climate change impacts  all of that if we're not paying special  attention to it you know we could be  making  three giant mistakes
 

